**Internal encoding:** Simple Dynamic String (SDS) up to 512 MB. Small integers use a shared object pool.

**Core commands:** `SET`, `GET`, `INCR`, `DECR`, `MGET`, `MSET`, `SETNX`, `SETEX`

**Time complexity:** O(1) for single-key ops, O(N) for multi-key

**Best for:** Session tokens, serialized JSON blobs, counters, distributed locks, feature flags

```java
// Atomic counter — no DB round-trip needed
redisTemplate.opsForValue().increment("page:views:" + pageId);

// Cache a serialized object with 30-min TTL
redisTemplate.opsForValue().set(
    "user:" + userId, userJson, Duration.ofMinutes(30)
);
```

**Memory tip:** Strings holding integers < 10,000 share a global object pool, so millions of small counters cost almost nothing extra.

**Internal encoding:** Listpack (< 128 fields) or hash table. Listpack saves ~10× memory over individual String keys.

**Core commands:** `HSET`, `HGET`, `HMSET`, `HGETALL`, `HDEL`, `HINCRBY`, `HEXISTS`

**Time complexity:** O(1) for single-field ops, O(N) for `HGETALL`

**Best for:** Object-shaped data (user profiles, product details), partial updates, structured caching

```java
// Store user profile as individual fields
HashOperations<String, String, String> hash = redisTemplate.opsForHash();
hash.put("user:42", "name", "Alice");
hash.put("user:42", "email", "alice@example.com");
hash.put("user:42", "loginCount", "0");

// Increment a single field without fetching the whole object
hash.increment("user:42", "loginCount", 1);

// Fetch only the fields you need
String name = hash.get("user:42", "name");
```

**Memory tip:** Keep field count under `hash-max-listpack-entries` (default 128) to stay in compact encoding. One Hash with 20 fields uses far less RAM than 20 separate String keys.

**Internal encoding:** Quicklist (linked list of listpacks) since Redis 7, giving O(1) ends with memory-efficient small segments.

**Core commands:** `LPUSH`, `RPUSH`, `LPOP`, `RPOP`, `LRANGE`, `LLEN`, `BRPOP`, `LINDEX`

**Time complexity:** O(1) push/pop, O(N) for index access or range

**Best for:** Message queues, activity feeds, recent-N lists, job queues

```java
// Simple work queue: producer pushes, consumer blocks
redisTemplate.opsForList().leftPush("jobs:email", jobJson);

// Blocking pop — consumer waits up to 30s for work
String job = redisTemplate.opsForList()
    .rightPop("jobs:email", Duration.ofSeconds(30));

// Recent activity feed — keep last 100 entries
redisTemplate.opsForList().leftPush("feed:user:42", activityJson);
redisTemplate.opsForList().trim("feed:user:42", 0, 99);
```

**Memory tip:** `LTRIM` after every push to cap list growth. Unbounded lists are the most common Redis memory leak in production.

**Internal encoding:** Intset (for small integer-only sets) or hash table.

**Core commands:** `SADD`, `SREM`, `SISMEMBER`, `SMEMBERS`, `SINTER`, `SUNION`, `SDIFF`, `SCARD`

**Time complexity:** O(1) for add/remove/membership, O(N) for full set ops

**Best for:** Tag systems, unique visitor tracking, mutual friends, deduplication

```java
// Track online users — uniqueness enforced automatically
redisTemplate.opsForSet().add("online:users", userId);
redisTemplate.opsForSet().remove("online:users", userId);

// Mutual friends between two users
Set<String> mutual = redisTemplate.opsForSet()
    .intersect("friends:alice", "friends:bob");

// Tags — find items tagged with BOTH "java" and "redis"
Set<String> both = redisTemplate.opsForSet()
    .intersect("tag:java", "tag:redis");
```

**Memory tip:** Sets with only small integers (< 512 elements, values < `set-max-intset-entries`) use the ultra-compact intset encoding.

**Internal encoding:** Skip list + hash table dual index. Skip list maintains order; hash table provides O(1) score lookups.

**Core commands:** `ZADD`, `ZINCRBY`, `ZRANGE`, `ZREVRANGE`, `ZRANK`, `ZREVRANK`, `ZSCORE`, `ZRANGEBYSCORE`

**Time complexity:** O(log N) for add/rank, O(log N + M) for range queries

**Best for:** Leaderboards, priority queues, time-series windows, rate limiting, scheduled tasks

```java
// Leaderboard — update score atomically
redisTemplate.opsForZSet().incrementScore("leaderboard:daily", playerId, points);

// Top 10 players (highest scores first)
Set<ZSetOperations.TypedTuple<String>> top10 = redisTemplate.opsForZSet()
    .reverseRangeWithScores("leaderboard:daily", 0, 9);

// Player's current rank (0-based from top)
Long rank = redisTemplate.opsForZSet()
    .reverseRank("leaderboard:daily", playerId);

// Sliding window — events in the last 60 seconds
long now = System.currentTimeMillis();
redisTemplate.opsForZSet()
    .removeRangeByScore("ratelimit:" + clientId, 0, now - 60_000);
```

**Memory tip:** Member strings dominate ZSet memory. Use short IDs as members and store metadata in a companion Hash.

**Internal encoding:** Radix tree of stream entries with consumer group metadata.

**Core commands:** `XADD`, `XREAD`, `XREADGROUP`, `XACK`, `XLEN`, `XRANGE`, `XTRIM`

**Time complexity:** O(1) for `XADD`, O(N) for range reads

**Best for:** Event sourcing, audit logs, lightweight message queues with consumer groups, change data capture

```java
// Produce an event
MapRecord<String, String, String> record = StreamRecords
    .newRecord()
    .in("orders:events")
    .ofMap(Map.of("orderId", "123", "event", "PLACED", "amount", "59.99"));
redisTemplate.opsForStream().add(record);

// Consumer group — create once
redisTemplate.opsForStream()
    .createGroup("orders:events", "order-processor");

// Consumer reads undelivered messages
List<MapRecord<String, String, String>> messages = redisTemplate.opsForStream()
    .read(Consumer.from("order-processor", "worker-1"),
          StreamReadOptions.empty().count(10).block(Duration.ofSeconds(5)),
          StreamOffset.create("orders:events", ReadOffset.lastConsumed()));

// Acknowledge after processing
messages.forEach(msg ->
    redisTemplate.opsForStream()
        .acknowledge("orders:events", "order-processor", msg.getId()));
```

**Memory tip:** Use `MAXLEN ~1000` on `XADD` or `XTRIM` to cap stream length and prevent unbounded growth.
